39/state.json" "file_sha256" "5eea7e91a9abc0d58092dce40dc68afaebe713a32d097ba5407f3506df1c80f1" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $4x-y-3=0$ passes through the points $U(0,-3)$ and $V(1,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $U$ and $V$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${U}^{′}(-12,-3)$ and ${V}^{′}(5,-1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =\\frac{-1-(-3)}{5-(-12)}(x-(-12)) \\\\ y+3 & =\\frac{2}{17}(x+12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{2}{17}(x+12) \\\\ 17y+51 & =2x+24 \\\\ 51-24 & =2x-17y \\\\ 2x-17y & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "5eea7e91a9abc0d58092dce40dc68afaebe713a32d097ba5407f3506df1c80f1" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :q