rt/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $3x+2y-6=0$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(2,0)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,-6)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,2).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{2-(-6)}{4-9}(x-9) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{8}{-5}(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =-\\frac{8}{5}(x-9) \\\\ 5y+30 & =-8x+72 \\\\ 8x+5y & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x+5y=42\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "5eea7e91a9abc0d58092dce40dc68afaebe713a32d097ba5407f3506df1c80f1" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "5eea7e91a9abc0d58092dce40dc68afaebe713a32d097ba5407f3506df1c80f1" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8x+5y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "5eea7e91a9abc0d58092dce40dc68afaebe713a32d097ba5407f3506df1c80f1" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B',$ the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6704.json" "file_sha256" "290cbab794266e393eb563de4b050cc13ebaab2aba4ccd812216c79830af05ec" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $B'.$ Therefore, $B'(3,-5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6704.json" "file_sha256" "290cbab794266e393eb563de4b050cc13ebaab2aba4ccd812216c79830af05ec" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6713.json" "file_sha256" "839860b0f95518b40d5286d9ea0def1513b90db98e96e6374d67f61bd9f8873f" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=3(0)-2=-2, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=3(1)-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =-6x \\\\ 6x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6713.json" "file_sha256" "839860b0f95518b40d5286d9ea0def1513b90db98e96e6374d67f61bd9f8873f" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6705.json" "file_sha256" "a5e9bd7dc476bdce501d3b2b13d35ca962969e96a980da0a7cba3de685886420" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y,$ we get $y=5x-3,$ and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=5(0)-3=-3, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=5(1)-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-(-3)}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{9}{2}(x+3) \\\\ 2y+12 & =9x+27 \\\\ 9x-2y+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6705.json" "file_sha256" "a5e9bd7dc476bdce501d3b2b13d35ca962969e96a980da0a7cba3de685886420" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $x=5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $x=5$ passes through the points $A(5,0)$ and $B(5,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-5,0)$ and ${B}^{′}(-4,1).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{1-0}{-4-(-5)}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y & =x+5 \\\\ x-y+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(3,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(16,7).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{7-(-1)}{16-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{8}{14}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-2) \\\\ 7y+7 & =4x-8 \\\\ 4x-7y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-7y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=-1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=-1$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(0,2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,3).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{3-2}{4-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-8 & =x \\\\ x-4y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-77726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S(-4,\\sqrt{3})$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $S:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${S}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${S}^{′}(15,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77726" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-77726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77726" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(12\\sqrt{3},-1)"] ["math" "(15,-1)"] ["math" "(15,-4\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(15,-7)"] ["math" "(5,-15)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_s