s of $F:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${F}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${F}^{′}(2,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,6)"] ["math" "(1,6)"] ["math" "(2,-4)"] ["math" "(2,6)"] ["math" "(6,-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2,6)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${A}^{′}$, the image of $A(-1,1)$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $A$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $A:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${A}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${A}^{′}(2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-77543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,0)"] ["math" "(-2,1)"] ["math" "(0,2)"] ["math" "(1,0)"] ["math" "(2,1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2,1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${K}^{′}$, the image of $K(\\sqrt{2},-2)$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $K$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $K:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${K}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${K}^{′}(-4,-8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-77725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-4,-12)"] ["math" "(-4,-8)"] ["math" "(-4,\\sqrt{2}-10)"] ["math" "(-8,-8)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2}-6,-8)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-4,-8)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=5x-2$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=5x-2$ passes through the points $A(0,-2)$ and $B(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(0,8)$ and ${B}^{′}(1,-15).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =\\frac{-15-8}{1-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-8 & =-23x \\\\ 23x+y-8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-77782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12x+y-15=0"] ["math" "13x+y-15=0"] ["math" "23x+y-8=0"] ["math" "2x+y-8=0"] ["math" "3x+y-8=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "23x+y-8=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=-x-4$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=-x-4$ passes through the points $W(0,-4)$ and $Z(1,-5)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $W$ and $Z$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${W}^{′}(8,0)$ and ${Z}^{′}(9,-3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${W}^{′}$ and ${Z}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{-3-0}{9-8}(x-8) \\\\ y & =-3(x-8) \\\\ y & =-3x+24 \\\\ 3x+y-24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3x+y+24=0"] ["math" "3x+y-24=0"] ["math" "3x+y-8=0"] ["math" "3x-y+24=0"] ["math" "3x-y+8=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x+y-24=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(3,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(16,7).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{7-(-1)}{16-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{8}{14}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-2) \\\\ 7y+7 & =4x-8 \\\\ 4x-7y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281236" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-7y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,3)$ and ${B}^{′}(5,4).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{4-3}{5-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-9 & =x-2 \\\\ x-3y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-281113" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281113" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-81.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-3$ passes through the points $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-5)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-3,-6)$ and ${B}^{′}(7,-13).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{-13+6}{7-(-3)}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =-\\frac{7}{10}(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10y+60 & =-7x-21 \\\\ 7x+10y & =-81\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7x+10y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $x=5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $x=5$ passes through the points $A(5,0)$ and $B(5,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-5,0)$ and ${B}^{′}(-4,1).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{1-0}{-4-(-5)}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y & =x+5 \\\\ x-y+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5x-y+10=0"] ["math" "x-5y+1=0"] ["math" "x-y+5=0"] ["math" "x=15"] ["math" "y=5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-77680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-y+5=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $4x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=27.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $4x-y-3=0$ passes through the points $U(0,-3)$ and $V(1,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $U$ and $V$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${U}^{′}(-12,-3)$ and ${V}^{′}(5,-1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =\\frac{-1-(-3)}{5-(-12)}(x-(-12)) \\\\ y+3 & =\\frac{2}{17}(x+12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{2}{17}(x+12) \\\\ 17y+51 & =2x+24 \\\\ 51-24 & =2x-17y \\\\ 2x-17y & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x-17y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=2$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=2$ passes through the points $P(0,2)$ and $Q(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(2,4)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{1-4}{4-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-3x+6 \\\\ 3x+2y & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-281582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x+2y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=-1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=-1$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(0,2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,3).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{3-2}{4-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-8 & =x \\\\ x-4y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-4y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $3x+2y-6=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=42.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $3x+2y-6=0$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(2,0)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,-6)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,2).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{2-(-6)}{4-9}(x-9) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{8}{-5}(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =-\\frac{8}{5}(x-9) \\\\ 5y+30 & =-8x+72 \\\\ 8x+5y & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x+5y=42\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56ee67948af06e735ec62e2c71c19a861377d7e439d94ff96a97a43ca2ea15a7"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8x+5y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ead81473406be18b00d676eaa00894d80b1a6328cc32414c2923178b7a83892"} {"question" "e-6704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/11b0a0434116475ce544b737e282fd34f252aa781a72e7bdedf1d868d47087c0.png)\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B',$ the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/example-6704.json" "file_sha256" "a6bb313c77eddbe642302af94b7cf0030424c5bc4cf6a32c52ba4c41db9a6d02"} {"question" "e-6704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/de0e0e242c55d338db39c80ce9b34c4a841aacef15a2368e1c00610ece9d2679.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $B'.$ Therefore, $B'(3,-5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/example-6704.json" "file_sha256" "a6bb313c77eddbe642302af94b7cf0030424c5bc4cf6a32c52ba4c41db9a6d02"} {"question" "e-6713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/3af646f67ee7157e5157ddd1d4296716e859c7d7c7c18d45037219ed4f75606e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/example-6713.json" "file_sha256" "6c688ac7e29dae79440ddfd8f0ba2df7071a3b82d222af5f2dcb3c014c820a64"} {"question" "e-6713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=3(0)-2=-2, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=3(1)-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/eaa950b7f0525109f33153dc28218222e1da17d7643281e9ac17776e87503784.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =-6x \\\\ 6x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/example-6713.json" "file_sha256" "6c688ac7e29dae79440ddfd8f0ba2df7071a3b82d222af5f2dcb3c014c820a64"} {"question" "e-6705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/3b0c64bc040716ca8ba42c78a229d1249ec7f80a3a4a584970863b20126c69db.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/example-6705.json" "file_sha256" "9120ce9f5f2a2177e55c24ecd9f40da4e7d3b7afc5d092a6b69c01d2e6373287"} {"question" "e-6705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y,$ we get $y=5x-3,$ and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=5(0)-3=-3, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=5(1)-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/assets/a32e0386507fdc40cc713854dde4ec413c2dfbb4c49a6e758e8690344f08459a.png)\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-(-3)}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{9}{2}(x+3) \\\\ 2y+12 & =9x+27 \\\\ 9x-2y+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14055459/example-6705.json" "file_sha256" "9120ce9f5f2a2177e55c24ecd9f40da4e7d3b7afc5d092a6b69c01d2e6373287"}] "usage" {"q-281130" [] "e-6704" [] "q-281583" [] "e-6705" [] "q-275719" [] "q-77680" [] "q-77722" [] "q-77779" [] "q-281236" [] "q-281580" [] "q-77726" [] "q-77543" [] "e-6713" []} "basis" 1818 "retractions" [[17592186152696 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $S^{′}$, the image of $S(-4, \\sqrt{3})$ under the action of $T$?"] [17592186151901 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B',$ the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$"] [17592186151901 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $B'.$ Therefore, $B'(3,-5).$"] [17592186151903 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$"] [17592186151903 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=3(0)-2=-2, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=3(1)-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =-6x \\\\ 6x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151902 :question/problem "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$"] [17592186151902 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y,$ we get $y=5x-3,$ and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=5(0)-3=-3, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=5(1)-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-(-3)}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{9}{2}(x+3) \\\\ 2y+12 & =9x+27 \\\\ 9x-2y+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186151063 17592186151064 17592186151065 17592186151066 17592186151067 17592186151081 17592186151082 17592186151083 17592186151084 17592186151085 17592186151087 17592186151088 17592186151089 17592186151090 17592186151091 17592186280930 17592186280944 17592186280951 17592186280958 17592186280959 17592186280960 17592186280961 17592186280962 17592186280972 17592186280973 17592186280974 17592186280975 17592186280976 17592186283248 17592186283251 17592186283252] "immutable_fields" [[17592186151062 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151062 :answer-field/id] [17592186151062 :answer-field/key] [17592186151062 :answer-field/type] [17592186151080 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151080 :answer-field/id] [17592186151080 :answer-field/key] [17592186151080 :answer-field/type] [17592186151086 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151086 :answer-field/id] [17592186151086 :answer-field/key] [17592186151086 :answer-field/type] [17592186280929 :answer-field/correct] [17592186280929 :answer-field/id] [17592186280929 :answer-field/key] [17592186280929 :answer-field/type] [17592186280943 :answer-field/correct] [17592186280943 :answer-field/id] [17592186280943 :answer-field/key] [17592186280943 :answer-field/type] [17592186280950 :answer-field/correct] [17592186280950 :answer-field/id] [17592186280950 :answer-field/key] [17592186280950 :answer-field/type] [17592186280957 :answer-field/correct] [17592186280957 :answer-field/id] [17592186280957 :answer-field/key] [17592186280957 :answer-field/type] [17592186280971 :answer-field/correct] [175921